**Justification.** Tree adjacency multiplication costs $O(N)$ per vector. Propagating $q$ columns and one offset for the marked-slice run costs $O(IN(q+1))$; each of $b(I+1)$ candidate rows can be tested against at most $q$ pivots with $O(q^2)$ work, and a $q$-dimensional solve costs $O(q^3)$. For the restricted-site run, ordinary dense column pivots cost $O(N^3)$, propagation of $r$ columns over $N$ epochs costs $O(N^2r)$, and $|S|N$ candidate rows cost $O(|S|Nr^2)$ to test. Solving costs $O(r^3)$. The named arrays implement those operations directly. These are sufficient finite constructions with exact arithmetic; no minimum work, traffic, width or storage is inferred. $\square$

